info  {"category":"agent-session","raw":"{\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-07T13:51:30.708Z\",\"type\":\"resp
      onse_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"custom_tool_call_output\",\"call_id\":\"call_DPvxAd85ubTNk
      1hNxUJ7RYi1\",\"output\":[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Script completed\\nWall time 0.1
      seconds\\nOutput:\\n\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Warning: truncated output
      (original token count: 11597)\\nTotal output lines: 434\\n\\n### plan headings\\n114: <h1>MSW
      Monitor</h1>\\n116: <div class=\\\"decision\\\"><strong>Core decision:</strong> build a
      monitor and launcher, not a graphical replacement for <code>msw</code>. Keep the status bar
      calm, expose frequent reversible actions directly, and hand credential-heavy, destructive, or
      parameter-heavy work back to the trusted CLI in Ghostty.</div>\\n119: <h2>1. What the
      repository tells us</h2>\\n121: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Three isolated trust
      domains</h3><p><code>dev</code> is the main workspace, <code>playgrounds</code> is for
      experiments, and <code>personal</code> is for personal projects. Each has its own VM,
      persistent ext4 workspace volume, Docker runtime volume, processes, credentials, network,
      loopback IP, and browser hostname.</p></section>\\n122: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Current resource limits</h3><p>Packaged defaults allocate 8 CPU /
      32&nbsp;GB to <code>dev</code>, 4 CPU / 32&nbsp;GB to <code>playgrounds</code>, and 6 CPU /
      16&nbsp;GB to <code>personal</code>. Resize ceilings are 12 CPU and 48/48/32&nbsp;GB
      respectively.</p></section>\\n123: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Safety
      boundary</h3><p>Guest GitHub access is read-only. Host-only pushes use a separate Keychain
      credential. Failed credential cleanup can quarantine a workspace; normal start, restart, exec,
      SSH, and push paths then fail closed.</p></section>\\n124: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Useful runtime facts</h3><p>MicroSandbox exposes stable JSON for
      status, inspect, and metrics. The current <code>msw status</code> table and <code>msw
      metrics</code> watch output are human-oriented and should not be parsed by the
      app.</p></section>\\n127: <h2>2. Recommended product shape</h2>\\n129: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Menu-bar icon</h3><p>One monochrome SF Symbol, such as
      <code>square.stack.3d.up</code>. Never put workspace names, CPU, memory, or action icons in
      the menu bar.</p><ul><li>Normal: neutral template icon.</li><li>Attention: small amber
      indicator.</li><li>Critical/offline: red indicator.</li><li>Busy: restrained progress
      treatment, never an attention-grabbing animation.</li></ul><p class=\\\"small\\\">Tooltip:
      “msw — all systems normal”, or the highest-priority issue.</p></section>\\n130: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Popover</h3><p>Use a compact, keyboard-navigable window around
      380–400&nbsp;pt wide. Header contains <strong>msw</strong>, aggregate state, Refresh,
      Settings, Activity, and Quit. Scroll only the workspace list; keep the header and footer
      stable.</p></section>\\n131: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Workspace cards</h3><p>One card
      per workspace, in fixed order. Show purpose, lifecycle state, last-updated time, CPU, memory,
      and the single best next action. Keep secondary actions in a labeled
      menu.</p></section>\\n132: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Detail window</h3><p>Open a normal
      window for data-rich work: Overview, Metrics, Logs, Repositories, Ports, and Maintenance.
      Recommended initial size: roughly 760×520&nbsp;pt. Do not turn the popover into a terminal
      emulator.</p></section>\\n135: <h3>Popover interaction</h3>\\n139: <li><strong>Starting /
      stopping / restarting:</strong> disable conflicting controls, show a labeled progress state
      such as “Starting dev…”, and refresh when the process exits.</li>\\n140:
      <li><strong>Unavailable:</strong> preserve the last good snapshot with a stale timestamp, show
      Retry, and leave other workspace cards usable.</li>\\n145: <h2>3. Complete command inventory
      and UI exposure</h2>\\n148: <h3>Core monitor and launcher</h3>\\n152: <tr><td><code>msw
      status</code> / <code>msw ps</code></td><td>Aggregate state + cards</td><td><span
      class=\\\"tag p0\\\">P0</span></td><td>Poll without starting VMs. Use a future JSON mode or
      direct read-only MicroSandbox JSON; never parse the ANSI/text wrapper.</td></tr>\\n153:
      <tr><td><code>msw start [TARGET]</code></td><td>Start button per card; Start all in
      overflow</td><td><span class=\\\"tag p0\\\">P0</span></td><td>Invoke the wrapper so quarantine
      checks, Keychain secret binding, readiness, and configured validation remain
      intact.</td></tr>\\n155: <tr><td><code>msw restart [TARGET]</code></td><td>Workspace
      menu</td><td><span class=\\\"tag p0\\\">P0</span></td><td>Confirmation plus progress. Never
      use direct <code>msb restart</code>; the wrapper rebinds the read credential
      correctly.</td></tr>\\n166: <h3>Repositories, GitHub, and pushes</h3>\\n172: <tr><td><code>msw
      pull WORKSPACE [PATH|all]</code></td><td>Repo action / Pull all in detail window</td><td><span
      class=\\\"tag p1\\\">P1</span></td><td>Show per-repository results. Explain fast-forward-only
      behavior and retain stderr for failures.</td></tr>\\n177: <tr><td><code>msw github setup
      WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO [--read-only]</code></td><td>Terminal handoff from Settings</td><td><span
      class=\\\"tag p2\\\">P2</span></td><td>Do not reimplement token entry initially. The CLI hides
      input, stores Keychain values, binds only the read secret, verifies the boundary, and rolls
      back on failure.</td></tr>\\n178: <tr><td><code>msw github remove
      WORKSPACE</code></td><td>Terminal handoff from Settings</td><td><span class=\\\"tag
      p2\\\">P2</span></td><td>Credential removal can quarantine a workspace if cleanup cannot be
      proven. Keep it out of the popover and require a terminal-visible operation.</td></tr>\\n182:
      <h3>Resources, maintenance, backup, and recovery</h3>\\n190: <tr><td><code>msw backup
      [DIRECTORY]</code></td><td>Backup wizard / progress window</td><td><span class=\\\"tag
      p1\\\">P1</span></td><td>Destination picker, sensitive-archive warning, list of currently
      running VMs, progress, checksum/info paths, and a clear note that running VMs are stopped then
      restored to their previous state.</td></tr>\\n191: <tr><td><code>msw restore ARCHIVE
      [--yes]</code></td><td>Recovery wizard only</td><td><span class=\\\"tag
      p2\\\">P2</span></td><td>Never put in the normal popover. Verify checksum and archive
      contents, show that managed state is replaced, require typed <code>RESTORE</code>, and state
      that VMs remain stopped and Keychain tokens are not changed.</td></tr>\\n196:
      <tr><td><code>msw host repair</code></td><td>Terminal handoff from Diagnostics</td><td><span
      class=\\\"tag p2\\\">P2</span></td><td>It changes loopback aliases, launch-daemon state,
      <code>/etc/hosts</code>, SSH authorization, and asks for <code>sudo</code>. Keep the privilege
      interaction in a terminal.</td></tr>\\n200: <h3>Reference commands and
      non-commands</h3>\\n207: <tr><td><code>./setup.sh</code> and its repair flags</td><td>Never a
      normal app action</td><td>Show installer guidance and a terminal fallback only.
      Rebuild/recreate/reset operations can affect persistent state.</td></tr>\\n213: <h2>4. SwiftUI
      and macOS architecture</h2>\\n214: <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> use a SwiftUI-first
      agent app targeting macOS 14+, with <code>MenuBarExtra</code> and
      <code>.menuBarExtraStyle(.window)</code>. This is Apple’s native path for a menu-bar-only
      utility with a popover-like window and standard SwiftUI controls. Keep the process and state
      boundaries Foundation-only so a tiny AppKit bridge can be added later if a concrete
      MenuBarExtra limitation appears.</p>\\n218: <div
      class=\\\"flow-box\\\"><strong>AppModel</strong><span class=\\\"small\\\">@MainActor
      state</span></div>\\n225: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>App shell</h3><p><code>@main</code>
      app with <code>MenuBarExtra</code>, <code>.menuBarExtraStyle(.window)</code>, an agent-style
      <code>LSUIElement</code> bundle setting, a Settings scene, and a separate detail window opened
      only when needed.</p></section>\\n226: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>State and
      concurrency</h3><p>Use an <code>@MainActor</code> AppModel backed by an <code>MSWClient</code>
      actor. Give each workspace an operation lock and a cancellable operation state. Status and
      metrics reads can run in parallel; conflicting writes cannot.</p></section>\\n227: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Process boundary</h3><p>Launch absolute executable paths with
      <code>Foundation.Process</code> and an argument array. Set a deterministic PATH containing
      <code>$HOME/.local/bin</code>, Homebrew, and system paths. Preserve <code>HOME</code> so
      <code>msw</code> finds <code>~/.config/msw/config.sh</code>.</p></section>\\n228: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Distribution</h3><p>For a personal utility, prefer a Developer
      ID/notarized distribution outside the Mac App Store and do not enable App Sandbox initially.
      The app must execute the user’s installed CLI, launch Ghostty/Zed, read selected files, and
      participate in normal Keychain prompts.</p></section>\\n231: <h3>Command adapter
      contract</h3>\\n232: <div class=\\\"callout success\\\"><strong>Current safe path:</strong>
      invoke <code>msw</code> for all policy-bearing mutations and terminal handoffs. For read-only
      telemetry, use <code>msb status --all --label msw.managed=true --format json</code>, <code>msb
      inspect NAME --format json</code>, <code>msb metrics --all --format json</code> or
      <code>--follow</code>, and <code>msw logs NAME --json --follow</code>. Do not parse <code>msw
      status</code> tables or <code>msw metrics --watch</code> terminal output.</div>\\n235:
      <li><code>msw status --format json</code>: schema version, MSW/MSB versions, workspace IDs,
      lifecycle state, resource configuration, hostnames/IPs, published ports, GitHub credential
      presence/access mode, verification metadata, quarantine state, capabilities, and timestamp.
      Never include token values.</li>\\n242: <h3>Suggested domain types</h3>\\n244:Lifecycle =
      running | stopped | starting | stopping | restarting | missing | unavailable\\n245:Freshness =
      current(observedAt) | stale(lastObservedAt) | unknown\\n247: id, purpose, host, ip, lifecycle,
      resources, metrics?, github?, quarantine?, freshness\\n252: <h2>5. Polling, freshness, and
      notifications</h2>\\n254: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Status cadence</h3><p>Read status
      immediately on launch and when the popover opens. Poll every 10–15 seconds while the app is
      active. Background polling must never call a command that starts a VM.</p></section>\\n255:
      <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Metrics cadence</h3><p>Show compact metrics from the latest
      snapshot. Stream or poll every 2–5 seconds only while the detail Metrics view is visible; stop
      the stream when it closes.</p></section>\\n256: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Stale
      data</h3><p>Every field has an observed-at timestamp. On failure, preserve the last good value
      with a visible Stale badge and Retry. Never convert a transport error into
      “Stopped”.</p></section>\\n257: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Notifications</h3><p>Request
      notification permission only from an explicit Settings toggle. Notify only after an initial
      baseline and only for deduplicated meaningful transitions or failed long-running operations.
      Suppress alerts caused by the app’s own action.</p></section>\\n260: <h2>6. Safety and UX
      guardrails</h2>\\n267: <tr><td>Quarantine bypass</td><td>Use <code>msw</code>, not direct
      <code>msb</code>, for mutations. Model quarantine separately from lifecycle state and do not
      offer a “clear quarantine” button.</td></tr>\\n272: <tr><td>Accessibility and
      localization</td><td>Use native
      Label/Button/Menu/Picker/TextField/SecureField/Toggle/ProgressView controls, visible text
      beside icons, VoiceOver labels that include workspace and state, keyboard focus order,
      semantic status text, and locale-aware FormatStyle APIs. Put strings in
      Localizable.xcstrings.</td></tr>\\n276: <h2>7. Delivery phases</h2>\\n277: <div
      class=\\\"step\\\"><div class=\\\"step-num\\\">0</div><div><h3>Stabilize the machine
      interface</h3><p>Add and test versioned JSON modes for status, metrics, GitHub status,
      repositories, and ports. Add fixture-based parser tests and preserve current human output.
      This is the prerequisite that prevents a brittle Swift text parser.</p></div></div>\\n278:
      <div class=\\\"step\\\"><div class=\\\"step-num\\\">1</div><div><h3>Ship the monitor
      core</h3><p>Create <code>macos/MSWMonitor</code> as a macOS 14+ Xcode app. Implement the
      status item, three cards, status/metrics polling, Start/Stop/Restart, Refresh, settings, login
      item toggle, errors, stale data, Activity, and terminal fallback.</p></div></div>\\n279: <div
      class=\\\"step\\\"><div class=\\\"step-num\\\">2</div><div><h3>Ship daily workflow
      launchers</h3><p>Add Open site, Copy URL, Open in Zed, Ghostty shell handoff, ports picker,
      streaming logs, repository picker, clone, pull, and GitHub status/verify. Add the detail
      window and bounded operation history.</p></div></div>\\n280: <div class=\\\"step\\\"><div
      class=\\\"step-num\\\">3</div><div><h3>Ship guarded maintenance</h3><p>Add disk inspection,
      resize, backup, tunnel tracking, deep check, push review, and identity settings. Keep
      operations visible with progress and failure recovery.</p></div></div>\\n281: <div
      class=\\\"step\\\"><div class=\\\"step-num\\\">4</div><div><h3>Ship recovery and expert
      controls</h3><p>Add secure native flows only where they improve safety: clean, upgrade,
      update, host repair, GitHub setup/remove, restore, and optional canned exec actions. Terminal
      handoff remains a supported escape hatch.</p></div></div>\\n283: <h2>8. Verification
      plan</h2>\\n285: <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Pure Swift tests</h3><ul><li>JSON decoding
      and schema-version rejection.</li><li>Lifecycle transition/state-machine
      invariants.</li><li>Stale/partial data aggregation and status-item severity.</li><li>Argument
      construction and path validation.</li><li>Redaction and bounded output
      buffers.</li><li>Confirmation policy for every dangerous command.</li></ul></section>\\n286:
      <section class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Process integration tests</h3><ul><li>Fake
      <code>msw</code>/<code>msb</code> executables with controlled stdout, stderr, exit codes,
      delays, and streamed JSON Lines.</li><li>Cancellation terminates child processes without
      launching duplicate operations.</li><li>Missing CLI/config, missing Keychain access,
      quarantine, and stale data all render recoverable states.</li></ul></section>\\n287: <section
      class=\\\"card\\\"><h3>Real Mac smoke test</h3><ul><li>Launch with all VMs stopped; verify no
      automatic starts.</li><li>Start <code>dev</code>, observe readiness and metrics, open a
      published site, open Zed and Ghostty, then stop.</li><li>Stream logs, run quick check, verify
      GitHub status, and exercise a failed command.</li><li>Test light/dark mode, VoiceOver,
      keyboard focus, notification opt-in, login item, signed build, and
      relaunch.</li></ul></section>\\n289: <div class=\\\"callout danger\\\"><strong>Do not use
      production backup/restore, credential rotation, force push, or destructive Docker cleanup as
      casual smoke tests.</strong> Cover their command planning and confirmation behavior with
      fakes; use a disposable workspace for real end-to-end validation.</div>\\n291: <h2>9.
      Decisions to keep fixed unless a concrete need appears</h2>\\n299: <li>Settings persist only
      non-sensitive preferences: paths, favorites, polling, notifications, launch-at-login, and
      display choices.</li>\\n302: <h2>Sources and evidence</h2>\\n303: <details
      open><summary>Repository and runtime sources read</summary><ul><li><code>README.md</code> —
      workspace purposes, defaults, daily use, trust
      boundary.</li><li><code>docs/MSW-CHEATSHEET.md</code> — complete command catalog and
      examples.</li><li><code>docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md</code> — lifecycle, ports, Git, backup, recovery,
      maintenance.</li><li><code>docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md</code> — Keychain split, read-only guest
      token, host-only push, verification, quarantine
      implications.</li><li><code>docs/TEST-REPORT.md</code> — 46 release scenarios and security
      invariants.</li><li><code>bin/msw</code>, <code>config.sh</code>, <code>setup.sh</code> —
      actual dispatch, side effects, environment, resources, and safety guards.</li><li>Installed
      <code>msb --help</code> and <code>msb --tree</code>; live read-only <code>msb status --all
      --format json</code> and <code>msb metrics --all --format json</code>
      output.</li></ul></details>\\n305: <details><summary>Research panel</summary><p>Seven
      independent <code>gpt-5.6-sol</code> research runs reviewed command inventory, SwiftUI/AppKit
      architecture, menu-bar UX, accessibility, safety, integration feasibility, and product
      prioritization. They made no repository code changes. Their convergent recommendations are
      reflected above: use <code>msw</code> for policy-bearing mutations, machine JSON for
      telemetry, progressive disclosure, explicit confirmations, stale-data semantics, and terminal
      fallback for sensitive workflows.</p></details>\\n\\n### msw behavior\\n9:# shellcheck
      source=/dev/null\\n187:quarantine_file() { printf '%s/%s.quarantine\\\\n'
      \\\"$GITHUB_META_DIR\\\" \\\"$1\\\"; }\\n193: if [[ \\\"$owner\\\" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && kill -0
