      ut":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1
      seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token
      count: 19606)\nTotal output lines: 1431\n\n### plan\n </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<main>\n
      <header class=\"hero\">\n <div class=\"eyebrow\">Product and technical plan ·
      2026-08-07</div>\n <h1>MSW Monitor</h1>\n <p class=\"lede\">A small native macOS menu-bar
      monitor and launcher for the three persistent MicroSandbox workspaces: <code>dev</code>,
      <code>playgrounds</code>, and <code>personal</code>.</p>\n <div
      class=\"decision\"><strong>Core decision:</strong> build a monitor and launcher, not a
      graphical replacement for <code>msw</code>. Keep the status bar calm, expose frequent
      reversible actions directly, and hand credential-heavy, destructive, or parameter-heavy work
      back to the trusted CLI in Ghostty.</div>\n </header>\n\n <h2>1. What the repository tells
      us</h2>\n <div class=\"grid\">\n <section class=\"card\"><h3>Three isolated trust
      domains</h3><p><code>dev</code> is the main workspace, <code>playgrounds</code> is for
      experiments, and <code>personal</code> is for personal projects. Each has its own VM,
      persistent ext4 workspace volume, Docker runtime volume, processes, credentials, network,
      loopback IP, and browser hostname.</p></section>\n <section class=\"card\"><h3>Current
      resource limits</h3><p>Packaged defaults allocate 8 CPU / 32&nbsp;GB to <code>dev</code>, 4
      CPU / 32&nbsp;GB to <code>playgrounds</code>, and 6 CPU / 16&nbsp;GB to <code>personal</code>.
      Resize ceilings are 12 CPU and 48/48/32&nbsp;GB respectively.</p></section>\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Safety boundary</h3><p>Guest GitHub access is read-only. Host-only pushes
      use a separate Keychain credential. Failed credential cleanup can quarantine a workspace;
      normal start, restart, exec, SSH, and push paths then fail closed.</p></section>\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Useful runtime facts</h3><p>MicroSandbox exposes stable JSON for status,
      inspect, and metrics. The current <code>msw status</code> table and <code>msw metrics</code>
      watch output are human-oriented and should not be parsed by the app.</p></section>\n
      </div>\n\n <h2>2. Recommended product shape</h2>\n <div class=\"grid\">\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Menu-bar icon</h3><p>One monochrome SF Symbol, such as
      <code>square.stack.3d.up</code>. Never put workspace names, CPU, memory, or action icons in
      the menu bar.</p><ul><li>Normal: neutral template icon.</li><li>Attention: small amber
      indicator.</li><li>Critical/offline: red indicator.</li><li>Busy: restrained progress
      treatment, never an attention-grabbing animation.</li></ul><p class=\"small\">Tooltip: “msw —
      all systems normal”, or the highest-priority issue.</p></section>\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Popover</h3><p>Use a compact, keyboard-navigable window around
      380–400&nbsp;pt wide. Header contains <strong>msw</strong>, aggregate state, Refresh,
      Settings, Activity, and Quit. Scroll only the workspace list; keep the header and footer
      stable.</p></section>\n <section class=\"card\"><h3>Workspace cards</h3><p>One card per
      workspace, in fixed order. Show purpose, lifecycle state, last-updated time, CPU, memory, and
      the single best next action. Keep secondary actions in a labeled menu.</p></section>\n
      <section class=\"card\"><h3>Detail window</h3><p>Open a normal window for data-rich work:
      Overview, Metrics, Logs, Repositories, Ports, and Maintenance. Recommended initial size:
      roughly 760×520&nbsp;pt. Do not turn the popover into a terminal emulator.</p></section>\n
      </div>\n\n <h3>Popover interaction</h3>\n <ul>\n <li><strong>Stopped:</strong> primary action
      is Start; show “Stopped” rather than treating it as an error.</li>\n
      <li><strong>Running:</strong> show CPU and memory bars; primary actions are Open site and Open
      in Zed, with Stop and Restart in the workspace menu.</li>\n <li><strong>Starting / stopping /
      restarting:</strong> disable conflicting controls, show a labeled progress state such as
      “Starting dev…”, and refresh when the process exits.</li>\n <li><strong>Unavailable:</strong>
      preserve the last good snapshot with a stale timestamp, show Retry, and leave other workspace
      cards usable.</li>\n <li><strong>Quarantined:</strong> show the stored reason prominently;
      disable SSH-derived actions and offer only GitHub repair / terminal fallback. Never clear the
      marker directly from the app.</li>\n <li>Do not automatically start stopped VMs during
      polling, app launch, metrics refresh, or opening the popover.</li>\n </ul>\n\n <h2>3. Complete
      command inventory and UI exposure</h2>\n <p>The matrix below covers every user-facing command
      in <code>msw help</code>. “Terminal handoff” is an intentional product decision, not a missing
      feature: the CLI already owns hidden prompts, Keychain access, SSH TTY behavior, transactional
      rollback, and exact confirmation phrases.</p>\n\n <h3>Core monitor and launcher</h3>\n <div
      class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Exposure</th><th>Tier</th><th>Recommended
      behavior</th></tr></thead>\n <tbody>\n <tr><td><code>msw status</code> / <code>msw
      ps</code></td><td>Aggregate state + cards</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Poll without starting VMs. Use a future JSON mode or direct read-only
      MicroSandbox JSON; never parse the ANSI/text wrapper.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw start
      [TARGET]</code></td><td>Start button per card; Start all in overflow</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Invoke the wrapper so quarantine checks, Keychain secret binding,
      readiness, and configured validation remain intact.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw stop
      [TARGET]</code></td><td>Stop in workspace menu; Stop all in overflow</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Graceful stop only. Confirm a single stop when work may be interrupted;
      require an explicit “Stop all” confirmation for the global action.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw restart [TARGET]</code></td><td>Workspace menu</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Confirmation plus progress. Never use direct <code>msb restart</code>;
      the wrapper rebinds the read credential correctly.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw metrics
      [TARGET]</code></td><td>Inline CPU/memory; charts in detail</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Use one-shot JSON for cards and a throttled stream only while Metrics
      is visible. Do not invoke the wrapper’s blocking <code>--watch</code> table.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw logs WORKSPACE [OPTIONS]</code></td><td>Logs tab / activity
      window</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Use <code>--json --follow --timestamps
      --no-color</code> and render a bounded ring buffer with search, copy, pause, and “Open in
      terminal”.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw open WORKSPACE [PORT] [SCHEME]</code></td><td>Open
      site button + port picker</td><td><span class=\"tag p0\">P0</span></td><td>Show the exact URL
      and that opening may start the VM. Favorites: 3000, 5173, 8080; allow any configured published
      port.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw url WORKSPACE [PORT] [SCHEME]</code></td><td>Copy link /
      Open link</td><td><span class=\"tag p0\">P0</span></td><td>Read-only URL construction. Do not
      imply that a service is listening; the configured port list is not a process
      probe.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw ports [TARGET]</code></td><td>Searchable port
      picker</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Show pre-published ports and fixed
      hostnames. Label this as “published”, not “active”.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw zed
      WORKSPACE [PATH]</code></td><td>Open in Zed from repo picker</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Use the wrapper and validate a workspace-relative path. If no path is
      chosen, open the workspace root.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw dev [PATH]</code>, <code>msw
      playgrounds [PATH]</code>, <code>msw personal [PATH]</code>, <code>msw shell WORKSPACE
      [PATH]</code></td><td>Open terminal / Ghostty handoff</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Launch a new Ghostty window with an argv-based command. Fallback: copy
      the exact command and offer a terminal handoff. Do not run an interactive SSH shell inside the
      app process.</td></tr>\n </tbody>\n </table></div>\n\n <h3>Repositories, GitHub, and
      pushes</h3>\n <div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Exposure</th><th>Tier</th><th>Recommended
      behavior</th></tr></thead>\n <tbody>\n <tr><td><code>msw repos
      WORKSPACE</code></td><td>Repositories tab / searchable picker</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p1\">P1</span></td><td>Load on demand because the wrapper starts a stopped VM. Cache paths
      with an observed-at timestamp.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw clone WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO
      [PATH]</code></td><td>Clone sheet</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Collect
      owner/repository and optional relative destination. Validate paths before execution; offer
      Open in Zed after success.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw pull WORKSPACE
      [PATH|all]</code></td><td>Repo action / Pull all in detail window</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p1\">P1</span></td><td>Show per-repository results. Explain fast-forward-only behavior and
      retain stderr for failures.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw push WORKSPACE PATH [--yes]
      [--force-with-lease]</code></td><td>Git action with review sheet</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p1\">P1</span></td><td>High-risk. Display repository, path, branch, exact commit, mode, and
      commits before approval. Preserve the native <code>PUSH</code> confirmation; never silently
      add <code>--yes</code>. Force-with-lease is a separate, stronger warning.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw identity \"NAME\" EMAIL [TARGET]</code></td><td>Settings / Git identity
      form</td><td><span class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>Explicit workspace/all scope. Show
      current values only after a deliberate read; do not make identity configuration part of the
      compact card.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw github status
      [WORKSPACE|all]</code></td><td>Credential-health badges</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p0\">P0</span></td><td>Show read access, host-write access, verification repository, and
      verification age. Never show token values.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw github verify
      WORKSPACE [REPO]</code></td><td>Verify access button in detail</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p1\">P1</span></td><td>Explain that it creates and deletes a temporary verification branch and
      uses the workspace lock. Stream progress; disable conflicting GitHub actions.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw github setup WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO [--read-only]</code></td><td>Terminal
      handoff from Settings</td><td><span class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>Do not reimplement
      token entry initially. The CLI hides input, stores Keychain values, binds only the read
      secret, verifies the boundary, and rolls back on failure.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw github
      remove WORKSPACE</code></td><td>Terminal handoff from Settings</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p2\">P2</span></td><td>Credential removal can quarantine a workspace if cleanup cannot be
      proven. Keep it out of the popover and require a terminal-visible operation.</td></tr>\n
      </tbody>\n </table></div>\n\n <h3>Resources, maintenance, backup, and recovery</h3>\n <div
      class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Exposure</th><th>Tier</th><th>Recommended
      behavior</th></tr></thead>\n <tbody>\n <tr><td><code>msw disk
      [TARGET]</code></td><td>On-demand Diagnostics section</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p1\">P1</span></td><td>Label clearly: it starts a stopped VM and runs guest <code>df</code>
      plus Docker usage. Show filesystem and Docker usage separately.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw
      resize WORKSPACE MEMORY [CPUS]</code></td><td>Resource sheet</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p2\">P2</span></td><td>Use steppers/sliders bounded by configured ceilings. Show aggregate
      host allocation and confirm changes that may pressure the Mac.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw
      clean [TARGET] [--volumes]</code></td><td>Advanced Maintenance only</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p2\">P2</span></td><td>Two-stage confirmation. Default cleanup preserves volumes; the volume
      checkbox is visually destructive and explains unused database volumes can be
      deleted.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw check [--deep]</code></td><td>Diagnostics: Quick check;
      Deep check behind disclosure</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Quick check is a
      useful read-only health signal. Deep check starts stopped VMs, probes SSH/internet/published
      ports, and creates temporary health services/containers; require explicit
      confirmation.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw backup [DIRECTORY]</code></td><td>Backup wizard /
      progress window</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Destination picker,
      sensitive-archive warning, list of currently running VMs, progress, checksum/info paths, and a
      clear note that running VMs are stopped then restored to their previous state.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw restore ARCHIVE [--yes]</code></td><td>Recovery wizard only</td><td><span
      class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>Never put in the normal popover. Verify checksum and
      archive contents, show that managed state is replaced, require typed <code>RESTORE</code>, and
      state that VMs remain stopped and Keychain tokens are not changed.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw upgrade [TARGET]</code></td><td>Terminal handoff / Advanced
      Maintenance</td><td><span class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>Long-running guest package
      upgrade. Show affected workspaces and require explicit approval; terminal output is the safest
      first implementation.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw update</code></td><td>Terminal handoff /
      Advanced Maintenance</td><td><span class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>Host MicroSandbox update
      plus <code>doctor</code>. Keep terminal-visible until a structured progress contract
      exists.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw tunnel WORKSPACE REMOTE [LOCAL]</code></td><td>Tunnel
      sheet + tracked Activity process</td><td><span class=\"tag p1\">P1</span></td><td>Ask for
      remote and local ports, display <code>http://localhost:LOCAL</code>, keep the SSH process
      alive, and provide Stop tunnel. Validate both ports.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw exec
      WORKSPACE COMMAND...</code></td><td>Terminal-first Advanced action</td><td><span class=\"tag
      p2\">P2</span></td><td>Do not embed an arbitrary shell in the popover. Offer canned commands
      and “Open terminal”; if a form is added later, pass argv without shell
      interpolation.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw host repair</code></td><td>Terminal handoff from
      Diagnostics</td><td><span class=\"tag p2\">P2</span></td><td>It changes loopback aliases,
      launch-daemon state, <code>/etc/hosts</code>, SSH authorization, and asks for
      <code>sudo</code>. Keep the privilege interaction in a terminal.</td></tr>\n </tbody>\n
      </table></div>\n\n <h3>Reference commands and non-commands</h3>\n <div
      class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Exposure</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr></thead>\n <tbody>\n
      <tr><td><code>msw version</code></td><td>Settings → Diagnostics</td><td>Show MSW and
      MicroSandbox versions; include in Copy Diagnostics.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw docs
      [TOPIC]</code></td><td>Settings → Documentation</td><td>Open the relevant installed guide in
      the default Markdown application or terminal. Topics are cheatsheet, setup, github, and
      tests.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>msw help</code></td><td>Settings →
      Documentation</td><td>Offer “Copy CLI help” rather than a second command catalog in the
      app.</td></tr>\n <tr><td><code>./setup.sh</code> and its repair flags</td><td>Never a normal
      app action</td><td>Show installer guidance and a terminal fallback only.
      Rebuild/recreate/reset operations can affect persistent state.</td></tr>\n
      <tr><td><code>__github-verify-transaction</code></td><td>Never expose</td><td>Internal
      transaction entry point; only the wrapper should invoke it.</td></tr>\n <tr><td>Raw
      <code>msb</code> image/volume/snapshot/remove/update commands</td><td>Never expose by
      default</td><td>They bypass MSW policy and trust-boundary orchestration. Keep them for expert
      terminal use only.</td></tr>\n </tbody>\n </table></div>\n\n <h2>4. SwiftUI and macOS
      architecture</h2>\n <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> use a SwiftUI-first agent app
      targeting macOS 14+, with <code>MenuBarExtra</code> and
      <code>.menuBarExtraStyle(.window)</code>. This is Apple’s native path for a menu-bar-only
      utility with a popover-like window and standard SwiftUI controls. Keep the process and state
      boundaries Foundation-only so a tiny AppKit bridge can be added later if a concrete
      MenuBarExtra limitation appears.</p>\n <div class=\"flow\" aria-label=\"Application
      architecture flow\">\n <div class=\"flow-box\"><strong>MenuBarExtra</strong><span
      class=\"small\">status icon + popover</span></div>\n <div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div>\n <div
      class=\"flow-box\"><strong>AppModel</strong><span class=\"small\">@MainActor
      state</span></div>\n <div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div>\n <div
      class=\"flow-box\"><strong>MSWClient</strong><span class=\"small\">actor + typed
      intents</span></div>\n <div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div>\n <div
      class=\"flow-box\"><strong>ProcessRunner</strong><span class=\"small\">argv, timeout,
      stderr</span></div>\n </div>\n <div class=\"grid\">\n <section class=\"card\"><h3>App
      shell</h3><p><code>@main</code> app with <code>MenuBarExtra</code>,
      <code>.menuBarExtraStyle(.window)</code>, an agent-style <code>LSUIElement</code> bundle
      setting, a Settings scene, and a separate detail window opened only when
      needed.</p></section>\n <section class=\"card\"><h3>State and concurrency</h3><p>Use an
      <code>@MainActor</code> AppModel backed by an <code>MSWClient</code> actor. Give each
      workspace an operation lock and a cancellable operation state. Status and metrics reads can
      run in parallel; conflicting writes cannot.</p></section>\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Process boundary</h3><p>Launch absolute executable paths with
      <code>Foundation.Process</code> and an argument array. Set a deterministic PATH containing
      <code>$HOME/.local/bin</code>, Homebrew, and system paths. Preserve <code>HOME</code> so
      <code>msw</code> finds <code>~/.config/msw/config.sh</code>.</p></section>\n <section
      class=\"card\"><h3>Distribution</h3><p>For a personal utility, prefer a Developer ID/notarized
      distribution outside the Mac App Store and do not enable App Sandbox initially. The app must
      execute the user’s installed CLI, launch Ghostty/Zed, read selected files, and participate in
      normal Keychain prompts.</p></section>\n </div>\n\n <h3>Command adapter contract</h3>\n <div
      class=\"callout success\"><strong>Current safe path:</strong> invoke <code>msw</code> for all
      policy-bearing mutations and terminal handoffs. For read-only telemetry, use <code>msb status
      --all --label msw.managed=true --format json</code>, <code>msb inspect NAME --format
      json</code>, <code>msb metrics --all --format json</code> or <code>--follow</code>, and
      <code>msw logs NAME --json --follow</code>. Do not parse <code>msw status</code> tables or
      <code>msw metrics --watch</code> terminal output.</div>\n <p>Add a small versioned
      machine-readable surface to <code>msw</code> before production UI work:</p>\n <ul>\n
      <li><code>msw status --format json</code>: schema version, MSW/MSB versions, workspace IDs,
      lifecycle state, resource configuration, hostnames/IPs, published ports, GitHub credential
      presence/access mode, verification metadata, quarantine state, capabilities, and timestamp.
      Never include token values.</li>\n <li><code>msw metrics [TARGET] --format json --once</code>
      and <code>--follow</code>: preserve the existing human default while exposing a stable JSON
      contract.</li>\n <li><code>msw github status --format json</code>, <code>msw repos WORKSPACE
      --format json</code>, and <code>msw ports [TARGET] --format json</code>.</li>\n <li>Later:
      structured preview/result modes for <code>push</code>, <code>backup</code>,
      <code>restore</code>, and <code>disk</code>. Until then, keep those workflows
      terminal-visible.</li>\n </ul>\n <p class=\"small\">This avoids sourcing or parsing
      <code>config.sh</code> in Swift and avoids coupling …9606 tokens truncated…ne:\n
